1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,000
In this video, I'm going to
cover a few additional hints

2
00:00:03,000 --> 00:00:04,545
for using Buildroot,

3
00:00:04,545 --> 00:00:06,240
talking about how to optimize

4
00:00:06,240 --> 00:00:07,740
your Buildroot
development workflow.

5
00:00:07,740 --> 00:00:09,000
These are things
that have come up in

6
00:00:09,000 --> 00:00:10,890
the past with students projects,

7
00:00:10,890 --> 00:00:13,150
especially students
using Buildroot.

8
00:00:13,150 --> 00:00:15,120
One thing that might be a

9
00:00:15,120 --> 00:00:16,530
little confusing when
you're looking at

10
00:00:16,530 --> 00:00:20,370
Buildroot dependencies
is, for instance,

11
00:00:20,370 --> 00:00:22,530
if you want to add
bash to Buildroot,

12
00:00:22,530 --> 00:00:26,625
you might notice that under
the shell and utilities menu,

13
00:00:26,625 --> 00:00:28,680
bashes are listed as an option.

14
00:00:28,680 --> 00:00:30,525
If you do a search for bash,

15
00:00:30,525 --> 00:00:32,220
you can learn more
about why this is

16
00:00:32,220 --> 00:00:35,295
the case by looking
closely at the output.

17
00:00:35,295 --> 00:00:37,400
If you're trying to
figure out why bash is

18
00:00:37,400 --> 00:00:39,140
missing from the shells list,

19
00:00:39,140 --> 00:00:40,820
even though it's shown
here being under

20
00:00:40,820 --> 00:00:42,920
target packages and
shells and utilities,

21
00:00:42,920 --> 00:00:44,150
what you need to pay

22
00:00:44,150 --> 00:00:46,340
attention to is this
depends on line.

23
00:00:46,340 --> 00:00:48,200
What this is telling you is that

24
00:00:48,200 --> 00:00:52,220
the bash package is
only going to be

25
00:00:52,220 --> 00:00:56,540
shown if BR2_PACKAGE_
BUSYBOX_ SHOW_OTHERS

26
00:00:56,540 --> 00:00:57,920
is set to true,

27
00:00:57,920 --> 00:01:00,215
and is currently set to false.

28
00:01:00,215 --> 00:01:02,150
The first thing you're
going to have to do

29
00:01:02,150 --> 00:01:04,885
in order to enable

30
00:01:04,885 --> 00:01:08,520
our C BR2__PACKAGE
bash as an option is

31
00:01:08,520 --> 00:01:12,555
to set the BR2_PACKAGE_
BUSYBOX_ SHOW_OTHERS.

32
00:01:12,555 --> 00:01:14,015
If you search for that,

33
00:01:14,015 --> 00:01:15,410
you'll see that
that's located under

34
00:01:15,410 --> 00:01:18,425
target packages and busybox.

35
00:01:18,425 --> 00:01:20,510
This is showing you

36
00:01:20,510 --> 00:01:22,370
the dependency that you
need to have before

37
00:01:22,370 --> 00:01:25,775
you're going to be able to
turn on at a target package.

38
00:01:25,775 --> 00:01:27,530
Pay attention to these
if you're having

39
00:01:27,530 --> 00:01:30,390
trouble locating a
specific package.

40
00:01:30,670 --> 00:01:35,660
Also wanted to mention some
tips about cross compiling.

41
00:01:35,660 --> 00:01:39,005
I think we've mentioned
this briefly before,

42
00:01:39,005 --> 00:01:43,265
but the way to optimize
your development workflow

43
00:01:43,265 --> 00:01:45,890
around making
frequent changes to

44
00:01:45,890 --> 00:01:49,270
source and recompiling
Buildroot accordingly.

45
00:01:49,270 --> 00:01:52,700
The way we've setup our
build so far with these

46
00:01:52,700 --> 00:01:56,285
commit hashes pointing to
specific commits in our repo,

47
00:01:56,285 --> 00:01:58,550
the kind of brute
force way to compile

48
00:01:58,550 --> 00:02:01,460
your source code would be
to push the version to git,

49
00:02:01,460 --> 00:02:03,260
copy this commit hash
and keep changing

50
00:02:03,260 --> 00:02:06,260
the version inside your make
file every time it changes.

51
00:02:06,260 --> 00:02:08,210
But this is going to
result in a lot of

52
00:02:08,210 --> 00:02:11,075
commits when debugging and
it's going to be pretty slow.

53
00:02:11,075 --> 00:02:14,045
Is there a better way? Of
course the answer is yes.

54
00:02:14,045 --> 00:02:19,995
You can use OVERRIDE_SRCDIR
as a way to avoid this issue.

55
00:02:19,995 --> 00:02:22,760
From the Buildroot
directory, you can run,

56
00:02:22,760 --> 00:02:26,025
make whatever your
package name is,

57
00:02:26,025 --> 00:02:27,710
OVERRIDE_SRCDIR,
and then point to

58
00:02:27,710 --> 00:02:30,230
wherever the location
is of your source.

59
00:02:30,230 --> 00:02:32,150
Then you can use the
same Buildroot commands

60
00:02:32,150 --> 00:02:34,230
to do assignment.

61
00:02:34,230 --> 00:02:37,065
For instance,
aesd-assignments-rebuild.

62
00:02:37,065 --> 00:02:39,050
What this is going
to do is ignore

63
00:02:39,050 --> 00:02:40,880
the version specified in
your assignments make

64
00:02:40,880 --> 00:02:44,150
file and instead use the
source from this directory,

65
00:02:44,150 --> 00:02:46,610
in this case the home directory.

66
00:02:46,610 --> 00:02:49,580
In order to use this
with a different module,

67
00:02:49,580 --> 00:02:51,380
you just use the
all caps version

68
00:02:51,380 --> 00:02:53,225
of whatever your module name is.

69
00:02:53,225 --> 00:02:55,355
Put all capital letters here

70
00:02:55,355 --> 00:02:57,940
and just add that
before OVERRIDE_SRCDIR.

71
00:02:57,940 --> 00:03:00,560
If you want to start from
an empty build output,

72
00:03:00,560 --> 00:03:02,810
you can say whatever
your package name is,

73
00:03:02,810 --> 00:03:07,090
dirclean to start out with
the clean build directory.

74
00:03:07,090 --> 00:03:10,250
Here's an example of when I use

75
00:03:10,250 --> 00:03:13,955
this OVERRIDE_SRCDIR command
with a rebuild all step.

76
00:03:13,955 --> 00:03:15,935
If you look carefully
at the output here,

77
00:03:15,935 --> 00:03:17,180
what you'll see is that it's

78
00:03:17,180 --> 00:03:20,425
synchronizing from a directory,

79
00:03:20,425 --> 00:03:22,520
in this case, my home directory

80
00:03:22,520 --> 00:03:24,365
that I specified
here with SRCDIR.

81
00:03:24,365 --> 00:03:26,405
It's going to use
the rsync command

82
00:03:26,405 --> 00:03:28,490
to keep the build directory

83
00:03:28,490 --> 00:03:30,530
used by Buildroot in
sync with the one

84
00:03:30,530 --> 00:03:33,310
that I've specified as
my OVERRIDE_SRCDIR.

85
00:03:33,310 --> 00:03:34,970
Anytime I make a change in

86
00:03:34,970 --> 00:03:36,950
my home directory and
then run this step,

87
00:03:36,950 --> 00:03:38,570
it'll copy any changes in,

88
00:03:38,570 --> 00:03:41,000
and do the build from

89
00:03:41,000 --> 00:03:46,720
the Buildroot output,
aesd-assignments-custom directory.